  "worked_solution": "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times Z\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}\u2282S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/9673951b4e72ba503e740369462f9e528e43ffc083706ee558ade7ccd52702f5.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{0}\u2282S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}={(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/797b66a293eb241f841869abe97e324f4301907f71029acceefd68487bef1b4f.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $\u2026,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},\u2026$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\u2026\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup \\text{\u00a0}{S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\u2026 \\\\  & ={\u22c3}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times Z$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/de19fcf0f246b09d4bb957568e026b36de3f55c1a6c7065d79faacee794a8326.png)",
